  <h1>India’s Waste Management Crisis — Policies and Way Forward</h1>
  <div><em>Indian Express | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Urbanisation and consumerism have created a massive waste management crisis in India. In 2024, Indian Express reported that most Indian cities are struggling with solid waste collection, segregation, and disposal. For UPSC, waste management illustrates the nexus of urban governance, environment, and public health.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Solid Waste Generated: ~160,000 tonnes/day (MoHUA, 2024).<br/>•	Collection Efficiency: ~95% in metros, <70% in small towns.<br/>•	Segregation at Source: <30% nationwide.<br/>•	Landfills: 3 major Delhi sites exceed safe capacity; similar crises in Mumbai, Chennai.<br/>•	Plastic Waste: ~3.5 million tonnes/year.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Challenges<br/>1.	Low Segregation: Lack of household-level awareness and enforcement.<br/>2.	Unscientific Disposal: Open dumping, landfill fires, groundwater contamination.<br/>3.	Financial Constraints: ULBs lack funds and manpower.<br/>4.	Informal Sector Neglect: Waste pickers remain unrecognised and underpaid.<br/>5.	Plastic Menace: Ban on single-use plastics weakly enforced.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Measures<br/>•	Solid Waste Management Rules (2016): Mandated segregation, composting, recycling.<br/>•	Plastic Waste Management Rules (2016, amended 2021): Ban on single-use plastics.<br/>•	Swachh Bharat Mission–Urban 2.0 (2021): Focus on waste segregation, processing.<br/>•	Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR): Producers accountable for plastic lifecycle.<br/>•	Waste-to-Energy Plants: Operational but face efficiency/viability issues.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>•	Awareness: Swachh Bharat improved civic consciousness.<br/>•	Digital Tools: GIS mapping, waste tracking in some smart cities.<br/>•	Private Participation: PPPs in recycling and composting.<br/>•	Circular Economy Push: Recycling startups gaining traction.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Persistent Gaps<br/>•	Implementation Deficit: Laws exist but poorly enforced.<br/>•	Land Scarcity: Cities lack space for new landfills.<br/>•	Behavioural Barriers: Citizen apathy toward segregation.<br/>•	Technology Challenges: Waste-to-energy plants face emissions issues.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Public Health: Unmanaged waste spreads disease.<br/>•	Environment: Landfills emit methane; plastics choke ecosystems.<br/>•	Economy: Recycling could create jobs and save resources.<br/>•	SDGs: Linked to SDG 11 (sustainable cities) and SDG 12 (responsible consumption).<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-III (Environment): Pollution, solid waste management.<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Role of ULBs, Swachh Bharat.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Waste management is about mindsets, not just technology.”<br/>o	“Urbanisation without waste planning leads to crisis.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Governance and urban policy.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Segregation Enforcement: Strict fines + citizen incentives.<br/>2.	ULB Empowerment: Financial autonomy, manpower, training.<br/>3.	Informal Sector Integration: Recognise waste pickers as essential workers.<br/>4.	Technology Use: Decentralised composting, recycling plants, biomethanation.<br/>5.	Behavioural Change: Education campaigns in schools and communities.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s waste management crisis is fundamentally a governance and behavioural challenge. Strong ULBs, citizen engagement, and sustainable technologies can turn waste into a resource. For UPSC aspirants, it reflects the intersection of environment, governance, and urbanisation.</p>
